            <ArticleTitle>Relationship between dysmenorrhea and posttraumatic stress disorder in Japanese high school students 9 months after the Great East Japan Earthquake.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>355-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2013.06.020</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-3188(13)00227-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the relationship between dysmenorrhea and posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) among Japanese adolescent girls 9 months after the Great East Japan Earthquake.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two high schools in Sendai, the largest city in northeastern Japan.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">1489 female adolescents aged between 15 and 18 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Impact of Event Scale-Revised (IES-R), intensity of menstrual pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The intensity of menstrual pain increased according to the comorbidity of PTSD (P &lt; .001). The IES-R subscale and total scores significantly increased according to the severity of menstrual pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study showed a significant association between natural disaster-induced PTSD and the severity of dysmenorrhea.</AbstractText>
